# InboxLabeler web application — configuration
#
# Copy to .env.local and fill in. .env.local is gitignored; this file is the
# committed template and must never hold a real value.
#
# Only the remote MCP endpoint and its OAuth flow read anything here. The
# existing UI and /api/labels need none of it and are unaffected if it is unset.
#
#   Variable               Development            Production
#   ---------------------- ---------------------- ------------------------------
#   MCP_PUBLIC_URL         optional               REQUIRED
#   OAUTH_SIGNING_SECRET   required               REQUIRED   ← secret
#   GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID       required to sign in    REQUIRED
#   GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET   required to sign in    REQUIRED   ← secret
#   DATABASE_URL           optional               REQUIRED   ← secret
#   DEV_DATABASE_DIR       optional               unused
#
# The three marked secret must be set as encrypted environment variables on the
# host — on Vercel, Project Settings → Environment Variables — and never
# committed, never printed, and never pasted into a support thread. The others
# are not secret but are still per-environment.
#
# Scope them to Production. A platform that gives every deployment its own
# hostname will otherwise run this authorization server at several addresses at
# once, all signing with the same key and writing to the same database, while the
# discovery documents name one. Give previews their own database branch, their own
# secret and their own Google client, or leave them unset — a deployment with no
# secrets refuses the requests that need them, which is the right answer. The
# OAuth and MCP endpoints also refuse any request whose Host is not the configured
# origin's, so a preview that did inherit production secrets still cannot run a
# flow.
#
# A production deployment missing any of its required variables refuses the
# requests that need them and writes the reason to stderr. It never falls back to
# a development default: the message goes to the log, never to the client.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where this deployment is reached
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The public origin, with no trailing slash. Every OAuth and MCP URL is derived
# from it: the issuer identifier, the MCP endpoint's resource identifier, and
# both discovery documents.
#
# It is configured rather than taken from the request's Host header on purpose.
# A token's audience and the issuer are values a client compares literally, and
# Host is attacker-controlled — deriving them from it would let a forged Host
# mint a token an honest client accepts for a different server.
#
# Optional in development, where it defaults to http://localhost:3000.
# Required in production. It must be the canonical origin clients will use —
# https://inboxlabeler.com — and not a per-deployment hostname: the issuer and a
# token's audience are compared literally, so a preview URL would mint tokens no
# client would accept at the real one.
MCP_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Token signing
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The secret access tokens are signed with, and the key the opaque per-user
# reference is derived from. At least 32 bytes; anything shorter is rejected
# rather than padded, because a stretched weak secret leaves tokens forgeable
# while the deployment looks configured.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 48
#
# Changing it invalidates every access token already issued, which is also the
# way to revoke them all at once.
OAUTH_SIGNING_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google, as the identity provider
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# InboxLabeler issues its own tokens for its own MCP endpoint, but does not
# authenticate people itself: it hands that one step to Google and gets back a
# stable account subject. Only the `openid` scope is requested — no email, no
# profile.
#
# Create an OAuth 2.0 Client ID of type "Web application" at
# https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials, then add, exactly:
#
#   Authorised JavaScript origin   https://inboxlabeler.com
#   Authorised redirect URI        https://inboxlabeler.com/oauth/callback
#
# and, for a local checkout, the development pair alongside them:
#
#   Authorised JavaScript origin   http://localhost:3000
#   Authorised redirect URI        http://localhost:3000/oauth/callback
#
# The redirect URI must match MCP_PUBLIC_URL, because the callback URL is derived
# from it rather than written down anywhere in the application. One Google client
# can hold both pairs, so development and production may share it — or not, which
# is tidier. GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET is a secret.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Durable storage
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Postgres, holding both hosted schemas: the OAuth flow state (registered
# clients, logins in progress, authorization codes, refresh tokens) and each
# user's own labels and match history. They share a connection and nothing else
# — separate tables, separate migrations. Access tokens are in neither: they are
# signed and self-contained, so /mcp validates one without a lookup.
#
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION, and a secret. Without it a hosted deployment refuses
# the requests that need it rather than falling back, because a store one restart
# or one extra instance can invalidate is the failure durability exists to remove.
#
# On Neon, use the POOLED connection string — the host containing "-pooler". Each
# serverless instance keeps its own small pool, so many instances against the
# direct endpoint would exhaust the compute's connection limit; the pooler exists
# for exactly that shape. The connection string Neon offers already carries
# `?sslmode=require`, which is what turns TLS on: keep it.
#
#   DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@ep-xxx-pooler.REGION.aws.neon.tech/inboxlabeler?sslmode=require
#
# Initialise or update the schema with:  npm run db:migrate
# DATABASE_URL=

# Development only: where the embedded database keeps its files (both schemas).
# Unset means in-memory, emptied when the process stops; set it to keep
# registrations, logins and labels across a restart the way production does.
# Ignored entirely when DATABASE_URL is set.
DEV_DATABASE_DIR=.devdb
